  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Travel conditions continue to improve for\nholiday travel as surface temperatures go above freezing\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nTravel conditions will continue to improve as we go through the\nevening hours and continue into the weekend. The icing threat\nhas ended from this event but can not rule out localized slick\nspots. \n\nAnother round of rain showers will move through the area tonight\nand continue into the early morning hours tomorrow but moving\nout during the later part of the morning. With little\naccumulation expected with those rain showers.\n\nConditions will continue to improve Saturday evening and into\nSunday. As no impactful weather is expected during that\ntimeframe. As another weather system will begin moving into the\narea early next week.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Another low pressure system is expected to \nimpact the area Monday, though track and impacts remain \nuncertain.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION... On Sunday, a deep upper level \ntrough will be centered over the Ohio Valley and will swing an \naccompanying low pressure towards Maine, strengthen as it tracks\nacross the northeast. The exact track of the system remains \nuncertain at this time, but model guidance suggests the low will\npass somewhere over Maine on Monday. Strong winds will be the \nprimary concern with this system, but the low location will \ndetermine how strong the winds will be. The latest forecast has \nshifted the low slightly north, and as a result, winds have \nreduced slightly over waters and and along the coast Monday \nafternoon. If the low is further north, winds will be weaker, \nand if the low is more off shore, winds will also be weaker and \nless onshore. If the low is just inland, the southeast winds \ncould produce some coastal flooding concerns. Precipitation will\nmainly be in the form of rain, with some snow mixing in across \nthe north. The timing and track of the low will become clearer \nas we get closer to the event.\n\nBehind the cold front, colder air aloft will allow strong winds\naloft to mix down to the surface through the day on Tuesday \nleading to elevated gusts across the area. Again, the timing and\ntrack of the upper level low will determine the strength of \nthese wind gusts possible solutions will become clearer as we \nget closer to the event.",
